Aim - Write a C program with a buffer overflow vulnerability, exploiting the vulnerability, and discussing mitigation strategies.

Theory - A buffer overflow is a vulnerability that occurs when a program writes more data into a fixed-size buffer than it can hold.
         The excess data may overwrite adjacent memory, causing program crashes, data corruption, or unintended program execution.
         gets() → fgets() — limits the number of characters read.
         strcpy() → strncpy() — allows control over the amount of data copied.
         strcat() → strncat() — helps prevent writing beyond the destination.
         sprintf() → snprintf() — limits the number of characters written.
         scanf("%s", ...) → scanf("%Ns", ...) — limits input length.

Tools Used - C Programming

Procedure:

1. Displaying String and Buffer Size using sizeof()
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(){
    char name[10];
    strcpy(name, "Amrit");
    printf("%s | Buffer size: %zu bytes\n",name,sizeof(name));
    return 0;
}

2. Demonstration of Buffer Overflow using strcpy()
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(){
    char name[10];
    strcpy(name,"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOP");
    printf("Value: %s\n", name); 
    return 0;
}

3. Demonstration of Buffer Overflow in a Structure
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
struct StudentData {
    char buffer [8];
    char status [8];
};
int main(){
struct StudentData data;
strcpy(data.status, "SAFE");
printf("BEFORE: Status = %s\n", data.status);
strcpy(data.buffer, "AAAAAAAAHACKED"); // oversized copy into buffer
printf("AFTER: Buffer = %s\n", data.buffer);
printf("AFTER: Status = %s\n", data.status);
return 0;
}

4. Restricted Input using scanf()
#include <stdio.h>
int main(){
    char username[10];
    printf("Enter username: ");
    scanf("%9s", username);  // Restricted to 9 chars + '\0'
    printf("Username: %s\n", username);
    return 0;
}

5. Safe String Input using fgets()
#include <stdio.h>
int main(){
    char username[10];
    printf("Enter username: ");
    fgets(username,sizeof(username),stdin);
    printf("Username: %s", username);
    return 0;
}

6. Demonstration of Memory Addresses and Buffer Overflow
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
struct StudentData {
    char buffer [8];
    char status [8];
};
int main(){
struct StudentData data;
strcpy(data.status, "SAFE");
printf("Address of buffer: %p\n", (void *)data.buffer);
printf("Address of status: %p\n", (void *)data.status);
strcpy(data.buffer, "AAAAAAAAHACKED");
printf("After overflow, Status = %s\n", data.status);
return 0;
}

7. Safe String Handling using snprintf()
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(){
    char buffer[10];
    snprintf(buffer,sizeof(buffer), "%s", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ");
    printf("Stored value: %s\n", buffer); 
    return 0;
}

Conclusion - The experiment helped us understand buffer handling and string operations in C.
             It demonstrated buffer overflow and different techniques for safe input and string handling.
